WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.743
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.743 --> 00:00:02.160
LEIGHA JARETT: In
this video, I'll

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:05.790
be showing you how to create
an experience management data

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:08.610
warehouse by transforming
and centralizing

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:11.160
your survey responses.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:12.600
My name is Leigha Jarett.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.770
And I'm a developer
advocate from Google Cloud.

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:21.120
Surveys are a powerful tool
for all different businesses.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:24.750
Survey platforms allow
organizations to easily collect

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:28.680
large amounts of data around
customer satisfaction, employee

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:32.189
happiness, brand
awareness, and more.

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:34.590
However, surveys
are often used once

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:36.660
to solve a specific
problem and then

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:39.330
the data is forgotten about.

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:41.340
Additionally, the
shape of the data

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:43.470
can be pretty difficult
to work with using

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:46.770
standard tools like SQL
or business intelligence

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:49.560
platforms.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:53.790
Here, I'll show you how to
use Cloud Dataprep by Trifacta

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:57.840
to transform and load survey
responses from Google Forms

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:01.050
into BigQuery, where you can
join them onto additional data

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:05.055
sets and perform deeper analysis
using tools like Looker or Data

00:01:05.055 --> 00:01:05.555
Studio.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.710
Let's imagine that I'm
performing market research

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:13.500
for my data analytics company.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:15.840
I sent out a Google
Forms survey that

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:17.490
asked respondents
questions about

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:20.340
their analytics knowledge.

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:23.310
Google Forms will automatically
push the responses

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:24.900
into a Google Sheet.

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:28.740
However, if I pushed the sheet
directly into my warehouse,

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:33.060
the format would make
it difficult to query.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:37.600
Let's take a closer look at
this checkbox grid question.

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:40.210
In the Google Sheet,
one row is recorded

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:44.560
for each response, where each
grid column is its own column.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:47.740
And the selections are
all in one cell, separated

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:50.750
by a semicolon.

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:53.170
Now, what we really
want is for one row

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:56.680
to represent each selection so
that we can easily do things

00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:59.740
like count the number of people
who selected each platform

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:02.710
project combination.

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:05.680
In Cloud Dataprep,
I've created a flow

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:10.150
that transforms and loads
my responses into BigQuery.

00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:11.920
I've connected the
Google Sheet that

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:15.610
contains my survey responses
and then created a recipe

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:18.490
to clean the column headers.

00:02:18.490 --> 00:02:22.270
Next, the data is pushed into
four different recipes, one

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:26.230
for each question type, which
transforms the responses based

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:28.820
on the format.

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:31.930
Let's take a deeper look at the
multiple-choice checkbox grid

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:33.640
question.

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:36.430
In my recipe, I can
preview the data

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:39.950
at each step in my
transformation process.

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:42.550
I've already deleted the
columns that I don't need.

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:48.130
And now I want to extract each
element from my selection list.

00:02:48.130 --> 00:02:50.590
First, I'm going to
extract the selections

00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:53.920
into their own
individual columns.

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:56.260
Next, I can combine
these columns

00:02:56.260 --> 00:03:00.580
into a single array
for each response.

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:02.620
Now that I have all
of my selections

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:06.910
in an array, what I need to
do is restructure this data

00:03:06.910 --> 00:03:12.430
so that each selection becomes
its own row in the database.

00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:13.570
And there we have it.

00:03:13.570 --> 00:03:16.330
My data is now un-nested.

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:18.400
I can repeat similar
transformations

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:22.442
for my other formats until
I have all of my data loaded

00:03:22.442 --> 00:03:23.025
into BigQuery.

00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:29.210
In BigQuery, I can
see that Trifacta

00:03:29.210 --> 00:03:33.290
has pushed those responses into
the four tables representing

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:36.950
my different types of questions.

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:40.100
I can preview the data and see
that it's now in the format

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:43.520
that I would expect it to make
it easier for doing analytics

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:45.320
on.

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:48.260
And since all this data is
centralized in my warehouse,

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:51.450
I can easily join it back
onto other data sources,

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:54.980
like the accounts
table from my CRM.

00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:57.470
In Looker, I've created
my own dashboard

00:03:57.470 --> 00:04:01.400
that surfaces insights about
this survey to my team.

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:04.910
Here, I can drill in and see
which respondents correspond

00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:08.240
to accounts already in
our CRM and directly

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:10.370
reach out to the account
owners to let them know.

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:15.800
I'm also going to
jump down and drill

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:18.800
into my group of users
who are data analysts

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:22.010
that leverage Python.

00:04:22.010 --> 00:04:25.130
I'm going to hop into Looker's
explore environment, where

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:28.760
I can easily now send this
list of users to Marketo

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:33.020
and target them for an
upcoming launch that we have.

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:36.290
And now you know how to create
an experience management data

00:04:36.290 --> 00:04:39.020
warehouse, where your
organization can take

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:41.180
direct action on the
insights that they

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:43.640
get from survey responses.

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:45.670
Thanks for watching.

